icted output and the true output.</p> <p data-svelte-h=3D"svelte-3kw9rm">No=
w let=E2=80=99s shift our focus to the accuracy curve. It represents the mo=
del=E2=80=99s accuracy over time. The accuracy curve begins at a low value =
and increases as training progresses. Accuracy measures the proportion of c=
orrectly classified instances. So, as the accuracy curve rises, it signifie=
s that the model is making more correct predictions.</p> <p data-svelte-h=
=3D"svelte-5msz0y">One notable difference between the curves is the smoothn=
ess and the presence of =E2=80=9Cplateaus=E2=80=9D on the accuracy curve. W=
hile the loss decreases smoothly, the plateaus on the accuracy curve indica=
te discrete jumps in accuracy instead of a continuous increase. This behavi=
or is attributed to how accuracy is measured. The loss can improve if the m=
odel=E2=80=99s output gets closer to the target, even if the final predicti=
on is still incorrect. Accuracy, however, only improves when the prediction=
 crosses the threshold to be correct.</p> <p data-svelte-h=3D"svelte-1v47fv=
i">For example, in a binary classifier distinguishing cats (0) from dogs (1=
), if the model predicts 0.3 for an image of a dog (true value 1), this is =
rounded to 0 and is an incorrect classification. If in the next step it pre=
dicts 0.4, it=E2=80=99s still incorrect. The loss will have decreased becau=
se 0.4 is closer to 1 than 0.3, but the accuracy remains unchanged, creatin=
g a plateau. The accuracy will only jump up when the model predicts a value=
 greater than 0.5 that gets rounded to 1.</p> <blockquote class=3D"tip"><p =
data-svelte-h=3D"svelte-q6j789"><strong>Characteristics of healthy curves:<=
